.TH X25519 2
.SH NAME
x25519, x25519base, ed25519pubkey, ed25519sign, ed25519verify \- elliptic curve key exchange and signatures on Curve25519
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <u.h>
.br
.B #include <libc.h>
.br
.B #include <mp.h>
.br
.B #include <libsec.h>
.PP
.B
uchar x25519base[32];
.PP
.B
int x25519(uchar out[32], uchar scalar[32], uchar point[32])
.PP
.B
void ed25519pubkey(uchar *pk, uchar *a)
.PP
.B
void ed25519sign(uchar *sig, uchar *msg, ulong len, uchar *a, uchar *pk)
.PP
.B
int ed25519verify(uchar *sig, uchar *msg, ulong len, uchar *pk)
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I X25519
is the function of that name in RFC 7748:
it sets
.I out
to the product of
.I scalar
and the point on Curve25519 whose u coordinate is
.IR point ,
each 32 bytes, little-endian.
It clamps a copy of
.I scalar
as the RFC says
and ignores the top bit of
.IR point ;
it changes neither argument.
The top bit of a result is always 0.
.I X25519
returns 0, or \-1 if the result is all zero, as for a point of low order,
whatever the scalar.
.PP
.I X25519base
is the base point, u = 9.
For a key exchange, each side fills a 32-byte secret with
.I genrandom
(see
.IR rand (2))
and sends
.I x25519
of the secret and
.IR x25519base ;
then
.I x25519
of its secret and the other side's public value
gives each the same shared secret,
which a key derivation function such as
.I hkdf_x
(see
.IR sechash (2))
should hash before use.
.PP
.I Ed25519pubkey
sets the 32-byte public key
.I pk
of the 32-byte secret key
.IR a ,
as RFC 8032, section 5.1.5, says.
.I Ed25519sign
sets
.I sig
to the 64-byte signature of the
.I len
bytes of
.I msg
by
.I a
and its public key
.IR pk .
.I Ed25519verify
returns 1 if
.I sig
is a signature of
.I msg
by the key whose public key is
.IR pk ,
and 0 if not.
It takes what Go's
.B crypto/ed25519
takes: a public key whose y coordinate is not reduced, or whose x is 0 with its sign bit set,
stands for the point it encodes, where RFC 8032's decoding refuses it;
the signature's R must be the canonical encoding of [S]B \- [k]A, and S must be less than L.
.PP
These functions keep no state, and procs may call them at once.
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/libsec/port/curve25519.c
.br
.B /sys/src/libsec/port/ed25519.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR rand (2),
.IR rsa (2),
.IR sechash (2)
.br
RFC 7748, Elliptic Curves for Security.
.br
RFC 8032, Edwards-Curve Digital Signature Algorithm (EdDSA).
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I X25519
sets the error string to
.B "x25519: low order point"
when it returns \-1.
